#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb20ecb-3cf6-58b5-bd9d-6b965a3762f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104257} {:answer/id #uuid "c4deb98e-cd6b-547a-acfc-ec16190f06c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104258} {:answer/id #uuid "d48460d1-1d9f-59d8-ad06-074f62eacc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104259} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0807b-7929-566e-938d-f243ec8608a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} :answer-field/id #uuid "840c6519-ee5d-5b70-b43a-17d5816bc817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273e570a-a467-5f2a-8bbc-0b66a3856ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49022" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf978c7-66b2-536c-bc0f-c66ec44d098e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293010} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f79fc-fcd6-53fd-9e57-3eb075462ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293011} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a2f88-7e52-526e-b7eb-a215c8ae05fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293012} {:answer/id #uuid "b40b9d3a-81f5-5efd-bfae-5d3f85c57737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293013} {:answer/id #uuid "22d9769c-872c-51f6-af5f-0e8b1fd36f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f79fc-fcd6-53fd-9e57-3eb075462ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293011} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef4f50c-dc6a-5c61-aa9b-9f3c102f3f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ceddcae-d555-5f79-8be6-8c64add94b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-49024" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} & =0 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=e$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,{e}^{2}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}({e}^{2}) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{(e)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,e)$ | $e$ | $(e,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=e,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=e$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf0b828-522c-5854-af67-20e99c73f09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293017} {:answer/id #uuid "935d0875-03e7-556a-b30f-f199defdd249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293018} {:answer/id #uuid "71e66523-89ce-50ce-a0e9-99c78eb8541a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293019} {:answer/id #uuid "a54917cb-f4a0-5130-b8b3-829a0cc0328d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293020} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9796bd-8c80-5229-8ee9-2e4f9f0fc277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e66523-89ce-50ce-a0e9-99c78eb8541a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293019} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ce1b99-1050-507a-8734-0b893720346f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66a1c6ee-aed3-5592-86e6-e5621e001888" :question/math-academy-id "q-49032" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2x+4}$ defined for $x\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x+4)-2({x}^{3})}{(2x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(-2,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(x+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(-2,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{4(-1)^{3}+12(-1)^{2}}{(2(-1)+4)^{2}}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{9}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}]}
